---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';

const title = 'PDR Repair Standards | Peak Country Greeley, CO';
const description = 'How Peak Country approaches PDR inspection, factory-paint preservation, vehicle access, protection, metal repair, reassembly, and quality checks.';
const pageSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  '@id': `${site.url}/repair-standards/#service`,
  name: 'Peak Country Paintless Dent Repair Standards',
  description,
  url: `${site.url}/repair-standards/`,
  provider: { '@id': `${site.url}/#business` },
  areaServed: ['Greeley, Colorado', 'Weld County, Colorado', 'Northern Colorado']
};
---
<Layout title={title} description={description} exactTitle pageSchema={pageSchema}>
  <header class="page-head standards-head">
    <div class="eyebrow">Vehicle care · Technician judgment · Clear expectations</div>
    <h1>Our Paintless Dent Repair Standards</h1>
    <p>Peak Country approaches auto hail repair and PDR as work on a complete vehicle. These standards explain how inspection, access, protection, metal repair, reassembly, and an honest final review fit together.</p>
    <div class="about-actions"><a class="button" href="/free-hail-inspection/">Request a Free Hail or Dent Inspection →</a><a class="text-link" href="/about/">Meet the technician behind Peak Country →</a></div>
  </header>

  <div class="standards-intro"><div class="section"><span>20+</span><p><strong>Years of hands-on Paintless Dent Repair experience</strong> inform the decisions behind every Peak Country inspection and repair in Greeley, Weld County, and Northern Colorado.</p></div></div>

  <article class="section standards-list">
    <section><div class="standards-number">01</div><div><div class="eyebrow">Start with an inspection</div><h2>Read the damage before choosing the repair.</h2><p>A proper PDR assessment uses professional reflection lighting to reveal the shape of each dent and the surrounding panel. The technician considers paint condition, dent depth, location, metal condition, body-line involvement, previous repairs, and access behind the panel. Ordinary outdoor light and photographs can hide shallow dents, crowns, and surface distortion.</p><p>The inspection should lead to realistic repairability guidance. A broad dent with intact paint may be suitable for PDR, while a sharp impact in stretched metal may have significant limits. Peak Country explains what can be seen, what access may be needed, and where further findings could change the plan.</p></div></section>

    <section><div class="standards-number">02</div><div><div class="eyebrow">Protect the factory finish</div><h2>Keep original paint when the damage allows it.</h2><p>Paintless dent repair gradually reshapes suitable metal without sanding, body filler, or repainting. When the original finish remains intact and stable, preserving it can avoid color matching and refinishing on an otherwise repairable panel. That is a practical reason to consider PDR for appropriate auto hail damage, door dings, minor dents, and creases.</p><p>Factory-paint preservation is not a promise for every dent. Cracked or missing paint, excessive metal stretch, severe deformation, and previous bodywork may make another method more appropriate. The condition of the vehicle decides whether a paintless approach makes sense.</p></div></section>

    <section><div class="standards-number">03</div><div><div class="eyebrow">Access the damage carefully</div><h2>Plan the tool path and necessary access.</h2><p>Professional PDR may require controlled access behind the damaged metal. Depending on the vehicle and panel, that can involve careful handling or removal of trim, lights, interior panels, headliners, liners, or related components. Bracing, glass, wiring, airbags, seals, and closed structural areas can limit where a tool can safely travel.</p><p>Disassembly should have a clear repair purpose. Peak Country considers available access before work begins and avoids removing components merely for convenience. If appropriate access cannot be created without unreasonable risk, that limitation belongs in the repair recommendation.</p></div></section>

    <section><div class="standards-number">04</div><div><div class="eyebrow">Protect the entire vehicle</div><h2>The visible dent is only one part of the job.</h2><p>Vehicle care includes protecting interiors and finished surfaces, handling trim responsibly, controlling tools around glass and paint, and keeping removed components organized. Clean working conditions matter because grit, loose hardware, and careless tool placement can create damage unrelated to the original dent.</p><p>Peak Country’s repair philosophy is to avoid unnecessary damage and treat the surrounding vehicle with the same attention as the panel being repaired. That means considering clips, seals, electronics, upholstery, adjacent paint, and the working environment throughout the job.</p></div></section>

    <section><div class="standards-number">05</div><div><div class="eyebrow">Repair the metal</div><h2>Use measured movement and continuous visual feedback.</h2><p>During PDR, specialized tools may apply controlled pressure from behind a panel, while exterior glue-pull techniques may be used in suitable situations. The technician reads reflection lines to understand low areas, high spots, crowns, and panel tension. The metal is moved gradually through many small adjustments rather than one aggressive push.</p><p>Technique changes with dent shape, panel material, body lines, access, and how the metal responds. This is where technician experience matters: tools supply force, but judgment determines placement, direction, timing, and when to stop. Customers should understand the method at a useful level without being given an unrealistic impression that every panel responds the same way.</p></div></section>

    <section><div class="standards-number">06</div><div><div class="eyebrow">Reassembly matters</div><h2>Return approved access components with care.</h2><p>Any trim, lights, interior panels, headliner areas, liners, or related components handled for approved access must be returned thoughtfully. Reassembly includes attention to applicable clips and fasteners, trim fit, connections, and the condition of the surrounding area.</p><p>The exact checks depend on what the repair required and what was handled. Peak Country does not claim procedures that are irrelevant to a particular vehicle. The standard is simpler: components involved in the repair should be treated as part of the job, not as an afterthought once the dent looks better.</p></div></section>

    <section><div class="standards-number">07</div><div><div class="eyebrow">Final quality check</div><h2>Review the repair under useful lighting.</h2><p>A final visual review uses reflection lighting and multiple viewing angles to assess the repaired area, surrounding panel flow, and any known limitation discussed before the work. The technician also reviews the areas and components involved in access and reassembly.</p><p>Quality inspection is not a claim that every trace of every dent can always be removed. It is a deliberate comparison between the repair plan, the panel’s response, and the realistic expectation established for that vehicle.</p></div></section>

    <section><div class="standards-number">08</div><div><div class="eyebrow">When PDR is not the right repair</div><h2>An honest recommendation can be another repair method.</h2><p>Peak Country may recommend conventional body repair or another approach when paint is significantly damaged, metal is excessively stretched, structural conditions make PDR inappropriate, or safe access makes appropriate PDR impossible. Severe creases, collision damage, torn metal, certain edge damage, and extensive previous repair can also change what is reasonable.</p><p>Saying no to PDR can be the experienced decision. The aim is to explain the evidence, acknowledge limitations, and help the vehicle owner understand why another repair method better fits the damage. Straight answers and realistic expectations are part of technician-led service.</p></div></section>
  </article>

  <section class="section standards-links"><div><div class="eyebrow">Related Peak Country Information</div><h2>Understand the repair before you schedule it.</h2></div><nav aria-label="Related repair information"><a href="/auto-hail-repair/">Auto Hail Repair →</a><a href="/paintless-dent-repair/">Paintless Dent Repair →</a><a href="/door-ding-repair/">Door Ding Repair →</a><a href="/process/">Inspection and Repair Process →</a></nav></section>
  <section class="about-cta"><div><div class="eyebrow">Mobile service · Greeley and Northern Colorado</div><h2>Request a Free Hail or Dent Inspection</h2><p>Start with the vehicle, the visible damage, and a clear conversation about repairability.</p><div class="about-actions"><a class="button" href="/free-hail-inspection/">Request a Free Hail or Dent Inspection →</a><a class="text-link" href={`tel:${site.phone}`}>Call {site.phoneDisplay} →</a></div></div></section>
</Layout>
